      "question": {
        ":question/id": "ccd7ae50-1c4e-5bcc-8b8b-d405f5154f0b",
        ":question/problem": "If $tan\u2061a=-2,$ then $tan\u2061(-a)=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=tan\u2061x.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{\u2218}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $tan\u2061x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ntan\u2061(-x)=-tan\u2061x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan\u2061(-a) & =-tan\u2061a \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
